<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="ch-palmer-penguins">
  <title>Differences amongst the Palmer penguins</title>

  <introduction>
    <blockquote>
      <p>"One can't be angry when one looks at a penguin."</p>
      <p>--- John Ruskin</p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      The Long Term Ecological Research Network (LTER) carries out research at Palmer station, Antarctica, including
      studies of penguin species on islands in the Palmer Archipelago.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Questions.</em> How do the species differ? How do penguin populations on the three studied islands differ?
    </p>
    <p><em>Sources.</em> The <c>palmerpenguins</c> data package and associated study documentation.</p>
    <p><em>Structure.</em> One raw dataset with 344 penguins and 17 variables, plus a cleaned dataset with 8 variables.</p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-palmer-overview">
    <title>Getting an overview of the penguin data</title>

    <p>
      Some of these data are available in the <c>palmerpenguins</c> package, including size measurements and isotopic
      signature information for Adelie, Chinstrap, and Gentoo penguins. Colour information is not included: Gentoo
      have a distinctive orange bill and white marking above the eyes, while Chinstrap have a narrow black chinstrap.
      Attractive drawings of the three species are shown on the package webpage.
    </p>

    <p>
      A good example of the underlying LTER research appears in Gorman et al. (2014).
    </p>

    <p>
      The Palmer Archipelago lies west of the Antarctic Peninsula, where warming linked to climate change has been
      rapid. According to Fountain et al. (2022), this is bad for Adelie penguins that do better farther east in the
      colder Weddell Sea, while Gentoo are less affected.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-palens0">
      <image source="images/PalEns0-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Four plots of the Palmer penguin dataset.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Chinstrap penguins and Torgersen observations are fewer in number. Body mass is right-skewed overall and
      flipper length is strongly positively associated with body mass.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-palens1">
      <image source="images/PalEns1-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Gentoo penguins highlighted across the ensemble of plots.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <figure xml:id="fig-palens2">
      <image source="images/PalEns2-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Dream Island penguins highlighted across the ensemble of plots.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Gentoo are only sampled on Biscoe and are generally heavier with longer flippers. Dream includes all
      Chinstrap plus some Adelie, with generally smaller body mass and flipper length.
    </p>

    <p>
      Penguins in this dataset were nesting pairs with at least one egg. The study documentation also provides
      detailed sampling context (Gorman et al., 2014), and estimated total penguin numbers at Antarctic sites over
      the years are available via the MAPPPD website described in Humphries et al. (2017).
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-palens3">
      <image source="images/PalEns3-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Penguins weighing no more than 3 kg highlighted across the same plots.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <figure xml:id="fig-palens5">
      <image source="images/PalEns5-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>
          Penguins weighing no more than 3 kg plotted beside others, comparing bill length and bill depth.
        </p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <figure xml:id="fig-palens6">
      <image source="images/PalEns6-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Dream Island penguins plotted beside others for bill length and bill depth.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      The highlighted subsets show how selection and ghostplot-style layering can expose subgroup structure across
      multiple linked views in static form.
    </p>

    <p>
      In interactive graphics, selecting cases in one view can immediately highlight them in all related views.
      Static ghostplot variants are less immediate, but follow the same principle: draw the full data in muted form,
      then overlay the selected subset in stronger colour.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-palmer-pcp">
    <title>Looking at several variables in one plot</title>

    <p>
      Parallel coordinate plots combine categorical and continuous variables and can reveal species separation
      patterns not obvious in pairwise views.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-palpcp1">
      <image source="images/Palpcp1-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Parallel coordinate plot of the Palmer penguin data.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Reordering categories and axes, plus reversing bill depth, reduces visual clutter and strengthens separation
      cues between species.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-palpcp2">
      <image source="images/Palpcp2-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Reordered parallel coordinate plot of the Palmer penguin data.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      The reordered view shows Chinstrap and Adelie as similar on several measurements but different on bill length,
      while Gentoo separate clearly on multiple variables (with bill depth interpreted as reversed in this display).
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-palscat3">
      <image source="images/Palscat3-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Scatterplots of bill depth with bill length and flipper length.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <figure xml:id="fig-scath">
      <image source="images/scatH-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>
          Scatterplots of bill length and bill depth by species and island, with all-penguin data ghosted in the
          background.
        </p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Faceted scatterplots confirm species-island structure and clarify within-group versus overall associations.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-palmer-raw">
    <title>Using raw data to get more information</title>

    <p>
      The <c>penguins_raw</c> data include enough detail to pair female and male measurements within nests and compare
      them directly on equal-scale axes.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-scatr">
      <image source="images/scatR-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>
          Male-versus-female measurements for four variables in each pair; green reference lines indicate equality.
        </p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Males are generally larger than their female partners. Combined male-female measurement patterns also reinforce
      species differences seen in earlier displays.
    </p>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        <em>Answers.</em> Gentoo penguins tend to be heavier, with longer flippers and shallower bills, and in this
        dataset they appear only on Biscoe. Adelie tend to have shorter bills. Chinstrap appear only on Dream. Males
        are usually larger than females.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Further questions.</em> Do isotope measurements in the raw data differ systematically by species? Are
        there notable year-to-year differences across the three study years?
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-palmer-takeaways">
      <title>Graphical takeaways</title>
      <ul>
        <li><p>Selection and highlighting are powerful for subgroup analysis across plot ensembles.</p></li>
        <li><p>Ordering choices are crucial for making parallel coordinate plots informative.</p></li>
        <li><p>When observations are paired, paired scatterplots reveal structure that unpaired views miss.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </subsection>
  </section>
</chapter>
